@Mahaximus_ I fixed it by transitioning to @OpenBot_ and downgrading my Grok Bot subscription.

I'm getting better outcomes than Grok Bot was able to provide by itself.

Unless something drastically changes along the way, I'm unlikely to go back to just Grok Bot.

The first week was rough. I pay for X Premium, SuperGrok and Cursor Pro+, and I used Grok Bot every day. Links inside the app didn't open, and the bot couldn't post to X for me. Scheduled tasks I'd deleted kept coming back. Triggers that worked on desktop did nothing on iPhone.

By October 9 I had 24 bugs in a two-part public report, each with a suggested fix. That night I sent the first invoice, $500 a bug plus $5,000 for one feature idea, $17,000 in all. I called Grok Bot a really good beta that was still a beta.

@pjvann

@alexmichael I've been slowly transitioning.

Now run a setup where Openbot does most of the heavy lifting and Grokbot just hovers over him as a supervisor.

My Openbot Chief of staff en my Grok Bot chief of staff send eachother messages, its quite funny.

@OpenBot_ I opened OpenBot on Arch Linux, but it couldn’t connect to Gemini. I could only get it to work from the terminal by OPENBOT_USE_SYSTEM_CLI=1 — only then could OpenBot connect.

I asked OpenBot to troubleshoot the issue, and bam—it worked! Haha, even GrokBot couldn’t handle this. 

